/**
\page org_mitk_views_diffusionpreprocessing Preprocessing of Diffusion Images

\section QmitkDiffusionImagingPreprocessingPagePreprocessing Preprocessing

The Preprocessing view inspects and edits diffusion-weighted images (DWIs) and other images: their
gradients and b-values, their volumes, and their header (geometry). Select the image in the
<b>Image</b> box at the top. Every action creates a new image below the selected one in the Data
Manager and leaves the selected image unchanged. Actions that need a DWI are disabled for other
images; flipping, cropping and resampling also work on 3D images with one value per voxel. If an
action cannot be carried out, a message says why.

\section QmitkDiffusionImagingPreprocessingPageGradients Gradients tab

The table lists the shells of the DWI: each b-value and the number of volumes acquired with it.
b-values are rounded to whole numbers; volumes whose b-value rounds to 0 are b0 volumes.

\li <b>Remove gradient volume</b> and <b>Extract gradient volume</b> remove the volume with the given
index from the DWI or copy it into a 3D image.
\li <b>Reduce number of gradients</b> keeps, per shell, the number of volumes entered in the
second column of the table; a number larger than the shell keeps all of its volumes. The kept
directions are spread evenly over the sphere: starting from the first ones, directions are swapped
at random as long as this increases the smallest angle between two kept directions (opposite
directions count as equal). The search always starts the same way, so it gives the same result
for the same image. With <b>first n</b> the first volumes of each shell are kept instead.
\li <b>Round b-values</b> rounds each b-value to the nearest multiple of the value in
<b>Sampling frame</b>, for example 1995 and 2005 to 2000 with a value of 100. b-values that round to
0 become b0 volumes.
\li <b>Flip gradients</b> negates the checked x, y and z components of all gradients,
<b>Reorder gradient dimensions</b> reorders the components (for example "y x z" swaps x and y),
and <b>Mirror gradients to half sphere</b> negates every gradient whose x component is negative.
The image data stay as they are.
\li <b>Show gradients</b> adds one point set per shell that shows the gradient directions around
the centre of the image, and a point for the b0 volumes.
\li <b>Clear rotation of gradients</b> is described in the section on gradient directions below.

The two text boxes list the gradients, one line per component and one column per volume:
<b>Original Gradients</b> as stored in the file, <b>Working Gradients</b> as all tools use them
(see below).

\section QmitkDiffusionImagingPreprocessingPageValues Image Values tab

\li <b>Average repetitions</b> averages the volumes whose gradients agree within the
<b>Merge radius</b>, for example repeated b0 volumes or repeated directions.
\li <b>Merge selected DWIs</b> appends the volumes of the DWI chosen in the box next to the button
to those of the selected DWI. Both need the same size, voxel size, origin and direction matrix.
The gradients are rescaled to the larger of the two reference b-values, so each volume keeps its
b-value.
\li <b>Flip axis</b> mirrors the image along the checked image axes (the voxel axes, not the world
axes) and keeps its geometry.
\li <b>Resample image</b> changes the voxel grid, given as a <b>Sampling factor</b> per axis (2
halves the voxel size), a <b>New image spacing</b> or a <b>New image size</b>, with the chosen
interpolation. Sizes are rounded down. The outer corner of the image and its orientation stay
where they are. DWIs are resampled volume by volume; their gradients do not change.
\li <b>Crop Image</b> removes the given numbers of voxels at the lower (left column) and upper
(right column) end of each image axis. The remaining voxels keep their position in space.

\section QmitkDiffusionImagingPreprocessingPageHeader Header tab

The tab shows voxel size, origin, direction matrix (one column per image axis) and, for DWIs, the
measurement frame of the selected image. <b>Apply new header information</b> creates an image with
the values that were edited; untouched values stay exactly as they were. Voxel sizes must be
positive, the columns of the direction matrix are normalized and must be orthogonal, and the
measurement frame must be invertible.

<b>Align to</b> creates a copy of the selected image with the origin of the image chosen in the box
next to it. Only the origin is taken over.

\section QmitkDiffusionImagingPreprocessingPageOther Other tab

<b>Extract baseline image</b> averages the b0 volumes of the DWI into one 3D image. With the check
box, all b0 volumes are copied into the time steps of a 3D+t image instead. A DWI without b0 volume
gives an error message.

\section QmitkDiffusionImagingPreprocessingPageConvention Gradient directions and image geometry

MITK Diffusion stores the working gradients of a DWI as directions in world (LPS) coordinates,
derived from the original gradients of the file with its measurement frame. All tools use the
working gradients. The tools that change the geometry or the gradients keep them consistent with
the image data:

\li <b>Flip axis</b> mirrors the gradients of a DWI along the same image axes as the data, i.e.
along the world directions of these axes.
\li <b>Apply new header information</b>: when the direction matrix of a DWI changes and
<b>Rotate gradients with the image</b> is checked (default), the gradients turn with the image and
keep their relation to the voxel grid, as FSL bvecs do; unchecked, they stay fixed in world
coordinates. An edited measurement frame is applied to the original gradients.
\li <b>Clear rotation of gradients</b> recomputes the gradients without the rotation of the image
matrix. It is available only for DWIs whose gradients include that rotation: NRRD files of earlier
MITK versions and DICOM data.
\li <b>Flip gradients</b> and <b>Reorder gradient dimensions</b> act on the world components of the
gradients. Reordering requires each of x, y and z once.

*/
